[{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q1","number":1,"question":"<p>For each of the equations below find the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span> when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=1+2^{-x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=20-2^{0.1x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=10+e^{-0.1x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=5-e^{0.001x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=21+5e^{-0.5x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=3-5\\times(\\frac12)^{-\\frac14x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=1+1=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=20-1=19\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=10+1=11\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=5-1=4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=21+5=26\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">f)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=3-5=-2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q2","number":2,"question":"<p>Find the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\" data-display=\"false\"></span> when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> for the following:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=\\frac{100}{1+e^{-\\frac12t}}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=\\frac{18}{1+e^{-0.1t}}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=\\frac{100}{1+1}=50\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=\\frac{18}{1+1}=9\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q3","number":3,"question":"<p>A population of bacteria is modelled with the equation</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"P=250e^{0.15t}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the population size and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the time in hours.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>State the size of the initial population.</p></li><li><p>Find the size of the population after 20 hours.</p></li><li><p>When does the population reach 1000 bacteria?</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=250\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=250e^{0.15\\times20}=5021\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (whole number as bacteria are discretely counted)</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1000=250e^{0.15t}\\Rightarrow4=e^{0.15t}\\Rightarrow\\ln4=0.15t\\Rightarrow\\frac1{0.15}\\ln4=t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=9.24\" data-display=\"false\"></span> hours (3 SF)</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q4","number":4,"question":"<p>For each of the models below, find the time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (hours) it takes for an amount of a substance <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (grams) to double from its initial amount, giving your answers in exact form.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=3\\times2^t\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=4e^{1.5t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=150\\times1.6^t\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=20+4e^{0.1t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"6=3\\times2^t\\Rightarrow2=2^t\\Rightarrow t=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span> hour.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"8=4\\times e^{1.5t}\\Rightarrow2=e^{1.5t}\\Rightarrow1.5t=\\ln2\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow t=\\frac23\\ln2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> hours.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"300=150\\times1.6^t\\Rightarrow2=1.6^t\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow t=\\log_{1.6}2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> hours.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"48=20+4e^{0.1t}\\Rightarrow7=e^{0.1t}\\Rightarrow0.1t=\\ln7\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow t=10\\ln7\" data-display=\"false\"></span> hours.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q5","number":5,"question":"<p>The quantity of radioactive isotope Uranium-235 <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span> grams over time may be modelled by the equation</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"y=1000\\times2^{-0.00142t}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is millions of years.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>State the initial mass of the Uranium-235</p></li><li><p>Show that the half-life of Uranium-235 is about 704 million years.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>Initially <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=1000\" data-display=\"false\"></span> grams.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac12=2^{-0.00142t}\\Rightarrow\\log_2\\frac12=-0.00142t\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow t=\\frac1{0.00142}=704.22535211\\ldots\\approx704\" data-display=\"false\"></span> million.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q6","number":6,"question":"<p>For each of the models below, find the initial value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and state what is happening to the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span> as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> Hence sketch each of the models.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=10+2e^{-0.3x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-5+7e^{-2x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=1.2-0.6e^{-7.6x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=0.2+1.8e^{2.3x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=10+2=12\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\\to\\infty,y\\to10\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/smq-1.26/solutions/assets/diagrams/s6a.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=-7+7=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\\to\\infty,y\\to-5\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/smq-1.26/solutions/assets/diagrams/s6b.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=1.2-0.6=0.6\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\\to\\infty,y\\to1.2\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/smq-1.26/solutions/assets/diagrams/s6c.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=0.2+1.8=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\\to\\infty,y\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/smq-1.26/solutions/assets/diagrams/s6d.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q7","number":7,"question":"<p>The graph below shows the temperature of a cup of coffee over time.<br> The room temperature is 22<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C.<br> The temperature, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C, after <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> minutes may be modelled by the equation</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"T=A+Be^{-\\frac15t}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/smq-1.26/questions/assets/diagrams/q7.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>State the initial temperature of the coffee.</p></li><li><p>Write down the equation of the horizontal asymptote.</p></li><li><p>Hence find the values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p>Suggest how the model may be refined for a cup of coffee that is initially 100<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C.</p></li><li><p>Suggest values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B\" data-display=\"false\"></span> for a cup of coffee cooling at the same rate but from initial temperature 97<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C in a room at 18<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>98<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=22\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=22,B=98-22=76\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>Change the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B\" data-display=\"false\"></span> to be 78.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=18\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B=79\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q8","number":8,"question":"<p>The height of a plant is given by the equation</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"h=\\frac{50}{1+4e^{-\\frac15t}}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the height in cm and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the number of weeks that have passed.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>According to this model state the initial height of the plant.</p></li><li><p>What height does this model predict in the long term for this plant?</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\frac{50}{1+4}=10\" data-display=\"false\"></span> cm.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty,y\\to\\frac{50}{1+0}=50\" data-display=\"false\"></span> cm.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q9","number":9,"question":"<p>The height of a child was recorded for the first seven years of their life. A model suggested for the child's height, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h\" data-display=\"false\"></span> cm, at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> years old is given by</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"h=130-80\\times1.45^{-x}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0\\le x\\le7\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Using the model:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>State the height of a newborn baby.</p></li><li><p>Find the height of a child after 18 months, giving your answer to the nearest millimetre.</p></li><li><p>Estimate the age of the child in months (to the nearest month) when their height is 1 m.</p></li><li><p>Explain why this model might be unsuitable for predicting the child's height as a teenager.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=130-80=50\" data-display=\"false\"></span> cm.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"130-80\\times1.45^{-1.5}=84.2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> cm.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=100\" data-display=\"false\"></span>,<br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"100=130-80\\times1.45^{-x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-30=-80\\times1.45^{-x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac38=1.45^{-x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-x=\\log_{1.45}(\\frac38)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=2.63973\\ldots\" data-display=\"false\"></span> years<br> 32 months.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\\to\\infty,h\\to130\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so it doesn't go over a height of 1.3m.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q10","number":10,"question":"<p>An investment of \u00a31200 is made at 5% compound interest per annum, with interest added to the balance only at the end of each complete year.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Create an equation to model the value of the investment over time.</p></li><li><p>Sketch the curve for your model, including labelling any points of intersections with axes.</p></li><li><p>Find the value of the investment after 4 years giving your answer to the nearest penny.</p></li><li><p>Find when the investment reaches more than \u00a32000.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=1200\\times1.05^t\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/smq-1.26/solutions/assets/diagrams/s10b.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>When <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=4,y=1200\\times1.05^4=\\pounds1458.61\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2000=1200\\times1.05^t\\Rightarrow\\frac53=1.05^t\\Rightarrow t=\\log_{1.05}\\frac53=10.469848430\\ldots\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> So end of year 11.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q11","number":11,"question":"<p>A fine for speeding is initially \u00a3<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Each week it is not paid, the fees increase exponentially. After 1 week the fine becomes \u00a3150 and after 2 weeks it becomes \u00a3225.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Calculate the initial fine, \u00a3<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p>Find a model which for this data, giving your answer in the form</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"C=A\\times r^t\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the cost, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the time (in weeks) and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are constant values to be found.</p></li><li><p>Use your model to find the cost of the fine in week 4.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{225}{150}=1.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{150}{1.5}=\\pounds100\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C=100\\times1.5^t\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C=100\\times1.5^4=\\pounds506.25\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q12","number":12,"question":"<p>The number of people using a social media platform is declining exponentially since its peak of popularity (at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>).<br> At 3 years and 6 years it had 180 and 144 million active users respectively.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the number of active users at its peak.</p></li><li><p>Hence find a model which fits the data, giving your answer in the form</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"N=A\\times r^{\\frac13t}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"N\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the number of active users, in millions, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the time, in years, and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are constant values to be found.</p></li><li><p>Use your model to predict the number of users after 10 years has passed.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{144}{180}=0.8\" data-display=\"false\"></span> per 3 years which pass<br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{180}{0.8}=225\" data-display=\"false\"></span> million active users initially.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=225\\times0.8^{\\frac13t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=225\\times0.8^{\\frac13\\times10}=106.9\" data-display=\"false\"></span> million active users.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q13","number":13,"question":"<p>A population of insects is known to be growing exponentially. The population 2 and 4 months into the data collection is 1440 and 3240 insects respectively.<br> A model for the data may be given by</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"P=A\\times r^t\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is population, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is time (in months) and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are constant values to be found.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r\" data-display=\"false\"></span> in the model.</p></li><li><p>Interpret the value of the constants <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r\" data-display=\"false\"></span> in context.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{3240}{1440}=2.25\" data-display=\"false\"></span> per two months which pass<br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{1440}{2.25}=640\" data-display=\"false\"></span> initially<br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2.25^{0.5}=1.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=640\\times1.5^t\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>640 is the initial population of insects, and 1.5 shows that the population 150% of its previous size as each month passes.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q14","number":14,"question":"<p>The amount of stock in a warehouse of a particular product <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, is known to be 12 000 units. After 5 months this is expected to have reduced by 9500.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find a model of the form</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"P=ae^{kt}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the number of months passed, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are constant values to be found.</p></li><li><p>How much stock is available after 2 months?</p></li><li><p>The company orders a delivery from the manufacturers when the stock drops to 500 units. After how many months should they make this order?</p></li><li><p>What does this model predict in the long term for the stock of this product? Comment on its reliability.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=12000\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(5,2500)\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2500=12000e^{5k}\\Rightarrow\\frac5{24}=e^{5k}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow5k=\\ln\\frac5{24}\\Rightarrow k=\\frac15\\ln\\frac5{24}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=12000e^{\\frac15\\ln\\frac5{24}t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=12000e^{2\\times\\frac15\\ln\\frac5{24}}=6407.44299507=6407\" data-display=\"false\"></span> items.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"500=12000e^{\\frac15\\ln\\frac5{24}t}\\Rightarrow\\frac1{24}=e^{\\frac15\\ln\\frac5{24}t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow\\frac1{24}=e^{\\frac15\\ln\\frac5{24}t}\\Rightarrow\\ln\\frac1{24}=\\frac15\\ln\\frac5{24}t\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow t=10.1301210642=10.1\" data-display=\"false\"></span> months (3 SF)</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty,P\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> which doesn't allow for any changes in patterns such as ordering more stock.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q15","number":15,"question":"<p>The amount, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\" data-display=\"false\"></span> milligrams, of a particular drug in a person's body after <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> hours may be given by a function of the form</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"C=Ae^{-kt}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Given that the initial dose taken is 200mg and in 4 hours the amount of drug has halved once, find the values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> giving your answer in exact form.</p></li><li><p>Hence, find the number of hours it takes for the drug to reduce to 50 mg in a person's body.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C=Ae^{-kt}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(0,200):A=200\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(4,100):100=200e^{-4k}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow\\frac12=e^{-4k}\\Rightarrow\\ln\\frac12=-4k\\Rightarrow k=-\\frac14\\ln\\frac12=\\frac14\\ln2\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C=200e^{-\\frac14\\ln(2)t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"50=200e^{-\\frac14\\ln(2)t}\\Rightarrow\\frac14=e^{-\\frac14\\ln(2)t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow\\ln\\frac14=-\\frac14\\ln(2)t\\Rightarrow-4\\ln\\frac14=\\ln(2)t\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow t=8\" data-display=\"false\"></span> hours.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q16","number":16,"question":"<p>The number <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\" data-display=\"false\"></span> of restaurants in a particular fast food chain in their <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n\" data-display=\"false\"></span>th year may be modelled by the function</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"P=Ae^{kn}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>In the first year (<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>), there were 1000 restaurants and in the second year there were 1400.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^k=1.4\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p>Find the exact value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>How many restaurants does the model predict in the 4th year?</p></li><li><p>How many whole years is it before there are 100 000 restaurants?</p></li><li><p>Explain why the model is invalid in the long-term.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1000=Ae^k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"14000=Ae^{2k}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> Giving <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{1400}{1000}=e^k\\Rightarrow e^k=1.4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k=\\ln1.4\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=\\frac{1000}{1.4}=\\frac{5000}{7}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=\\frac{5000}{7}e^{(\\ln1.4)n}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=\\frac{5000}{7}e^{4\\ln1.4}=2744\" data-display=\"false\"></span> restaurants.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"100000=\\frac{5000}{7}e^{\\ln1.4n}\\Rightarrow140=e^{\\ln1.4n}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow\\ln140=n\\ln1.4\\Rightarrow n=14.6866\\ldots\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so 15 years.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty,P\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span> There can't be infinite restaurants.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q17","number":17,"question":"<p>A substance <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is known to decay exponentially according to the model</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"p=p_0e^{-kt}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is time in years, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p_0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the initial quantity and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is an unknown constant.<br> Find the amount of time needed for the quantity to have reduced by 99%, giving your answer in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac1k\\ln A\" data-display=\"false\"></span> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is a positive integer.</p>","steps":["<p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0.01p_0=p_0e^{-kt}\\Rightarrow0.01=e^{-kt}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow\\ln0.01=-kt\\Rightarrow t=-\\frac1k\\ln0.01=\\frac1k\\ln100\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q18","number":18,"question":"<p>The amount of a drug <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span> mg in the blood stream is modelled by the equation</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"y=Ae^{-kt}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the time in hours since a tablet was taken. After 2 hours there was 500mg in the blood stream, and after 7 hours there was 88mg in the blood stream.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the exact value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p>Find the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\" data-display=\"false\"></span> to 1 decimal place.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(2,500):500=Ae^{-2k}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(7,88):88=Ae^{-7k}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{500}{88}=\\frac{Ae^{-2k}}{Ae^{-7k}}\\Rightarrow\\frac{125}{22}=e^{5k}\\Rightarrow\\ln\\frac{125}{22}=5k\\Rightarrow k=\\frac15\\ln\\frac{125}{22}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>Which gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"500=Ae^{-\\frac25\\ln\\frac{125}{22}}\\Rightarrow A=\\frac{500}{e^{-\\frac25\\ln\\frac{125}{22}}}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow A=1001.762885\\ldots\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=1001.8\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q19","number":19,"question":"<p>The populations of two types of fish in a lake may be modelled with the following equations:<br> <u>Fish A:</u></p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"P_A=ae^{0.08t}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p><u>Fish B:</u></p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"P_B=be^{0.12t}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P_A\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P_B\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are the respective populations of fish A and fish B and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the number of months passed since the start of the year.<br> The total number of fish in the lake at the start of the year is 85, then after 6 months passes the total population is 153.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b\" data-display=\"false\"></span> giving your answers to 1 decimal place.</p></li><li><p>Interpret what your values for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b\" data-display=\"false\"></span> mean in context.</p></li><li><p>Find the number of months passed when the population of fish B first exceeds the population of fish A.</p></li><li><p>Explain why the models are invalid in the long-term.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a+b=85\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"ae^{0.08\\times6}+be^{0.12\\times6}=153\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"ae^{0.48}+(85-a)e^{0.72}=153\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"ae^{0.48}+85e^{0.72}-ae^{0.72}=153\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"ae^{0.48}-ae^{0.72}=153-85e^{0.72}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a(e^{0.48}-e^{0.72})=153-85e^{0.72}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=\\frac{153-85e^{0.72}}{e^{0.48}-e^{0.72}}=49.3358921682\\ldots=49.3\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b=35.7\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>The approximate initial number of each type of fish.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"49.3e^{0.08t}=35.7e^{0.12t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{49.3}{35.7}=\\frac{e^{0.12t}}{e^{0.08t}}=e^{0.04t}\\Rightarrow0.04t=\\ln(\\frac{49.3}{35.7})\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow t=\\frac1{0.04}\\ln(\\frac{49.3}{35.7})=8.06933480658\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> After 8.07 months (3 SF)</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>Both models predict infinite exponential growth but the fish populations will be limited by the size of the lake or may be affected by disease or predators.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q20","number":20,"question":"<p>A beaker of liquid is cooling. Its initial temperature is 65<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C.<br> After 3 minutes its temperature is 35<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C.<br> The temperature is modelled using the equation</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"T=17+ae^{-kt}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the temperature in <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the time in minutes and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> are constants.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Find the exact values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>What is the temperature of the liquid after 5 minutes?</p></li><li><p>What is the temperature of the liquid in the long term?</p></li><li><p>State how the model could be modified if the room temperature is reduced.</p></li></ol><p>The temperature of a second liquid, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C, cooling from 80<span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C at the same point in time in the same room is modelled by the equation</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"y=A+be^{-1.5kt}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> has the same value from before.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"5\"><li><p>Find the time at which both liquids have the same temperature.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T=17+ae^{-kt}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(0,65):65=17+a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=48\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T=17+48e^{-kt}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(3,35):35=17+48e^{-3k}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> So <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"18=48e^{-3k}\\Rightarrow\\frac38=e^{-3k}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow\\ln\\frac38=-3k\\Rightarrow k=-\\frac13\\ln\\frac38=\\frac13\\ln(\\frac83)\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T=17+48e^{-\\frac13\\ln(\\frac83)t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T=17+48e^{-\\frac53\\ln(\\frac{24}{7})}=26.4^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C (3 SF)</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty,T\\to17^\\circ\" data-display=\"false\"></span>C.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>The value ``17'' in the model would be reduced and the ``48'' would increase by the same amount.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=17+63e^{-\\frac12\\ln(\\frac{24}{9})t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"17+63e^{-\\frac12\\ln(\\frac{24}{9})t}=17+48e^{-\\frac13\\ln(\\frac{24}{9})t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{63}{48}=\\frac{e^{-\\frac13\\ln(\\frac{24}{9})t}}{e^{-\\frac12\\ln(\\frac{24}{9})t}}\\Rightarrow\\frac{63}{48}=e^{\\frac16\\ln(\\frac{24}{9})t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow\\ln\\frac{63}{48}=\\frac16\\ln(\\frac{24}{9})t\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow t=\\frac{\\ln\\frac{63}{48}}{\\frac16\\ln(\\frac{24}{9})}=1.66349269039\\ldots\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=1.66\" data-display=\"false\"></span> minutes (3SF)</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q21","number":21,"question":"<p><strong>Prerequisite knowledge for Q21-Q26: Differentiation of the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"ae^{kx}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></strong> For each of the equations calculate the rate of change in <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\" data-display=\"false\"></span> when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=20+2e^{-0.3x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=4(5+e^{0.2x})\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y=70-60e^{-0.8x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{dy}{dx}=-0.6e^{-0.3x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x=2}=-0.6e^{-0.6}=-0.329\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (3 SF)</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{dy}{dx}=4\\times-0.2e^{0.2x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x=2}=0.8e^{0.4}=1.19\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (3 SF)</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{dy}{dx}=-48e^{-0.8x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x=2}=48e^{-1.6}=9.69\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (3 SF)</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q22","number":22,"question":"<p>The population of red squirrels in the UK can be modelled by the equation</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"P=3500e^{-\\frac1{225}\\ln(\\frac{700}{57})t}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is their population (in thousands) and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the number of years since 1800.<br> Show that in 2000, according to the model, the amount of squirrels is decreasing at a rate of approximately 4.2 thousand per year.</p>","steps":["<p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{dP}{dt}=-\\frac{140}{9}\\ln(\\frac{700}{57})e^{-\\frac1{225}\\ln(\\frac{700}{57})t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left.\\frac{dP}{dt}\\right|_{x=200}=-\\frac{140}{9}\\ln(\\frac{700}{57})e^{-\\frac{200}{225}\\ln(\\frac{700}{57})}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"=-4.19778030561\" data-display=\"false\"></span> thousand per year<br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\approx-4.2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> thousand per year.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q23","number":23,"question":"<p>A volume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"V\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (ml) of liquid being poured from a bottle may be modelled by the equation</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"V=330(1-e^{-0.1t})\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>after <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> seconds.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>State the theoretical maximum amount of liquid the bottle is holding, according to the model.</p></li><li><p>Find the rate at which the liquid is being poured when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> seconds.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty,V\\to330\" data-display=\"false\"></span>ml.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{dV}{dt}=33e^{-0.1t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left.\\frac{dV}{dt}\\right|_{x=5}=33e^{-0.5}=20.0155117705\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> 20.0 ml/s (3 SF)</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q24","number":24,"question":"<p>The amount of stock in a shop of two products <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\" data-display=\"false\"></span> may be modelled by the following equations where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the number of months since the start of the year.<br> <u>Product A:</u>   <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=100(1+e^{-kt})\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <u>Product B:</u>   <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P=400e^{-kt}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\ge0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is a positive constant.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Sketch the two models on the same axes, include labelling points where the curves meet the axes and any asymptotes.</p></li><li><p>State the amount of each product in the long term according to the model.</p></li><li><p>Explain the limitations of the model.</p></li><li><p>Show that the rate of decrease of product B is always 4 times that of product A.</p></li><li><p>Find the exact value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> given there is the same amount of each product after two months have passed.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/smq-1.26/solutions/assets/diagrams/s24a.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>Amount of product A <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\to100\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> Amount of product B <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>In both cases the models don't actually reach these values, and as the amount of each product is discrete the model only approximates at each point.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p>A: <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{dP}{dt}=-100ke^{-kx}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> B: <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{dP}{dt}=-400ke^{-kx}=4\\times-100ke^{-kx}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"100(1+e^{-2k})=400e^{-2k}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow100+100e^{-2k}=400e^{-2k}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow100=300e^{-2k}\\Rightarrow\\frac13=e^{-2k}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\Rightarrow\\ln\\frac13=-2k\\Rightarrow k=-\\frac12\\ln\\frac13=\\frac12\\ln3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q25","number":25,"question":"<p>The height of a tree is given by the equation</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"h=\\frac{18e^{\\frac12t}}{e^{\\frac12t}+3}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the height in metres and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is the number of years that have passed since it is planted. The graph of height against time is shown below.</p><figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/smq-1.26/questions/assets/diagrams/q25.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>According to this model state the initial height of the tree.</p></li><li><p>Show that</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"h=\\frac{18}{1+3e^{-\\frac12t}}\" data-display=\"true\"></span></li><li><p>What height does this model predict in the long term for this tree?</p></li></ol><p>A second tree was planted at the same time, and it may be modelled with equation</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"h=a-14e^{-\\frac12t}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is an unknown constant.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"4\"><li><p>Find the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> given this tree has a height of 2 m when planted.</p></li><li><p>Find the rate of growth of the second tree at 5 years.</p></li><li><p>State which of the two trees is expected to be taller.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\frac{18}{1+3}=4.5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> m.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"h=\\frac{18e^{\\frac12t}}{e^{\\frac12t}+3}\\times\\frac{e^{-\\frac12t}}{e^{-\\frac12t}}=\\frac{18}{1+3e^{-\\frac12t}}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> as req.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty,h\\to18\" data-display=\"false\"></span> m.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2=a-14\\Rightarrow a=16\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">e)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{dh}{dt}=7e^{-\\frac12t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left.\\frac{dh}{dt}\\right|_{t=5}=7e^{-\\frac52}=0.575\" data-display=\"false\"></span> metres per year (3 sf)</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">f)</h3><p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\\to\\infty,h\\to16\" data-display=\"false\"></span> m. So the first tree is expected to be taller.</p>"]},{"id":"smq-year1-1.26-q26","number":26,"question":"<p>Two models of mass <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"m\" data-display=\"false\"></span> kg over an exponentially decaying substance over time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\" data-display=\"false\"></span> days are given by</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"m=100e^{-0.05t}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>and</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"m=10+90e^{-kt}\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is a positive constant.<br> Find the ranges of values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> for which the second model is initially decaying at a faster rate than the first.</p>","steps":["<p>First model: <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{dm}{dt}=-5e^{-0.05t}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left.\\frac{dm}{dt}\\right|_{t=0}=-5\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> Second model: <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{dm}{dt}=-90ke^{-kt}\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left.\\frac{dm}{dt}\\right|_{t=0}=-90k\" data-display=\"false\"></span><br> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-90k&gt;-5\" data-display=\"false\"></span> so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k&gt;\\frac1{18}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]}]